Subject: venus: one queue of each family on macOS

Each queue a device is created with is one the host's driver makes. NVIDIA's (GB10, 580) takes about 150 ms to create
a queue and 40 ms to destroy one: a device with the 16 queues of its first family takes 2.6 s to create, one with a
single queue 0.33 s. The Metal layer asks for every queue a family offers, in every process that makes a Metal device,
so each application spent its first seconds there with its main thread waiting. One queue of a family is offered;
VN_QUEUES_PER_FAMILY=n offers up to n.

--- a/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_physical_device.c
+++ b/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_physical_device.c
@@ -1005,6 +1005,21 @@
       }
    }
 
+#if DETECT_OS_APPLE
+   /* A queue asked for is a queue the host driver makes, and some take long over each: NVIDIA's 150 ms to create and
+    * 40 ms to destroy (a device with the 16 queues of its first family: 2.6 s). An application that takes every queue
+    * on offer pays that at each start, so one queue of a family is offered (VN_QUEUES_PER_FAMILY=n for more).
+    */
+   {
+      const char *limit_str = os_get_option("VN_QUEUES_PER_FAMILY");
+      const uint32_t limit = limit_str && atoi(limit_str) > 0 ? (uint32_t)atoi(limit_str) : 1;
+      for (uint32_t i = 0; i < count; i++) {
+         if (qfp[i].queueFamilyProperties.queueCount > limit)
+            qfp[i].queueFamilyProperties.queueCount = limit;
+      }
+   }
+#endif
+
    if (VN_DEBUG(NO_SPARSE))
       physical_dev->sparse_binding_disabled = true;
 
